BMW 328 made its debut at the race track in June 14th, 1936. 34 runners were listed in[ the] sports car category of the International Eifel Race. Seven of these were entered in the 2-litre class, and five were BMWs. Four were “Typ 319/1” cars, the other Ernst Jakob Henne’s snow-[white] Roadster. The car stood out from the crowd, its body[ boasting] far more flowing forms, a curved[ front] end with a pair of slim,[ kidney]-[shaped] air intakes similar to those of the BMW 326 presented at the Berlin Motor Show that spring, headlights integrated into the front wings, a low,[ sl]oping windscreen[ and] a bulbous[ rear] end. Out of sight, yet most certainly[ not] out of[ mind] after the car’s lap[ times] in practice, was the new engine[ lurking beneath the] bonnet[.] The sound rumbling to the surface[ through] the bonnet’s two leather securing belts indicated[ the] presence[ of a] six-cylinder unit producing maybe 80 or even 90 horsepower[.]
